WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:08.240
In Linux, there is a unifying design
philosophy—everything is a file.

2
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.330
The operating system uses the exact same
interface, whether it is handling a regular

3
00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:20.360
file, a directory, character and block
devices, a pipe, a FIFO, or a network socket.

4
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.790
As users, we interact with all these
elements using familiar, human-readable text

5
00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:31.630
names, like notas.txt. But if you look
inside the core metadata structure of any

6
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:37.310
file, the inode, you will notice a surprising
omission—the file's name is completely

7
00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:42.470
absent. If the file itself does not contain
its own name, where exactly does that

8
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:47.230
text live? And how does the kernel actually
find the physical data on the disk when

9
00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.750
you ask for it? To the kernel, a file's
true identity is its inode, which is

10
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.140
recognized strictly by a unique number.

11
00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:59.900
Unix systems deliberately separate
a file's content from its metadata.

12
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:05.680
Inside this inode structure, the kernel
stores the file type, access permissions,

13
00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:12.020
owner, total size, and four specific
timestamps—access, modify, change, and birth.

14
00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:15.080
Notice that there is no
field dedicated to a name.

15
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:20.290
The inode also holds extents—these are
pointers that tell the file system exactly

16
00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:25.210
where the contiguous runs of 4-kilobyte
data blocks live on the physical ext4 disk

17
00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:29.820
partition. The missing name
actually lives inside a directory.

18
00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:34.290
A directory is simply another file, but
its data is formatted as a list of text

19
00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.180
names paired with their
corresponding inode numbers.

20
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:42.310
It also includes an entry for itself,
denoted by a dot, and its parent, denoted by

21
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.230
two dots. A directory acts purely as a
map—it translates human-friendly text

22
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.310
strings into the numerical inode identifiers
that the kernel requires to fetch your

23
00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.370
data. Let us trace the exact steps the
kernel takes to resolve an absolute path,

24
00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.760
like /home/student/notas.txt.

25
00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:02.590
The process begins at the root inode.

26
00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.830
The kernel reads this directory's table,
scans the text entries, and looks up the

27
00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.160
target inode number for the string home.

28
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.090
It then moves to the home inode, reads
its directory table, and locates the entry

29
00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:20.460
for student. Entering the student inode,
the kernel reads one final table.

30
00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:25.740
Here it finds notas.txt, and
extracts the target inode number.

31
00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:30.670
This component-by-component traversal is
the mandatory bridge connecting the user

32
00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.740
space abstraction of a path to
the physical reality of the disk.

33
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:39.370
Because the name is completely detached
from the physical file data, a single file

34
00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.680
can have multiple independent names.

35
00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:47.420
This is a hard link—a new directory entry
pointing directly to the exact same inode.

36
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:52.460
The inode tracks how many names point
to it using the st_nlink counter.

37
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.750
The kernel only frees the data blocks when
this drops to zero, and no process has

38
00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.960
the file open. A symbolic
link operates differently.

39
00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:04.730
As we see in this terminal output, a
symbolic link is a completely new inode.

40
00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:10.070
The output shows atajo has
a distinct inode number,

41
00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:16.240
12190687, separate from the
original file's 12190686.

42
00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.500
Its data content is just a text
path pointing to another location.

43
00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:25.750
The Linux file system operates not as a
rigid set of folders, but as a flexible web

44
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.840
of pointers linking human-readable
names to physical identities.

45
00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.620
Let's review. Does an inode
contain the file's name?

46
00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:39.500
No, the name lives in the directory
entry, which points to the inode.

47
00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:43.000
What is the difference between
a hard link and a symbolic link?

48
00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.650
A hard link points directly to the same
inode, whereas a symbolic link is a separate

49
00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:51.330
inode whose content is a path.

50
00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:57.040
Now open animation A3.1 in the
course hub, and run lab TD2.
